1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:04,259
Small popcorn, five dollars. Large, nine. Most people pick the small.

2
00:00:04,509 --> 00:00:06,877
Then the theater adds a medium, for eight dollars.

3
00:00:07,377 --> 00:00:10,683
Suddenly the large looks like a steal. Only one dollar more!

4
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:13,707
That medium isn't really there to be bought. It's a decoy.

5
00:00:14,207 --> 00:00:19,753
In a famous classroom test, students picked a magazine deal: web only, or print plus web for more.

6
00:00:20,003 --> 00:00:23,737
Web only: about sixty dollars. Print plus web: one twenty-five.

7
00:00:23,987 --> 00:00:25,459
Most picked the cheaper web deal.

8
00:00:25,709 --> 00:00:30,189
Then a silly print-only option was added, at the same price as print plus web.

9
00:00:30,439 --> 00:00:33,681
Nobody chose it. But now most students picked print plus web.

10
00:00:34,181 --> 00:00:38,512
It was a small classroom test, and later studies found decoys don't always work.

11
00:00:38,762 --> 00:00:41,151
These popcorn prices are just an example.

12
00:00:41,651 --> 00:00:46,259
So Tony added a medium soda. Small, two. Medium, three fifty. Large, four.

13
00:00:46,509 --> 00:00:48,088
Guess which one everybody ordered?

14
00:00:48,588 --> 00:00:52,129
That's the end of part five. Follow for more sneaky business tricks.

15
00:00:52,379 --> 00:00:56,326
And next time you see three sizes, ask: which one do they want me to buy?

